      <groundspeak:encoded_hints>FGL.9.8&#39; N OF S CURB 78 AVENUE

ASC BRASS TABLET FLUSH WITH GROUND.9.8&#39; NORTH OF THE SOUTH CURB OF 78 AVENUE</groundspeak:encoded_hints>
      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="687082915">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-19T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP253 Edmonton CPR

&lt;strong&gt;Posted:&lt;/strong&gt; N53° 30.852' W113° 29.654'

&lt;strong&gt;MyGPSr:&lt;/strong&gt; N53° 30.854' W113° 29.657'&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I woke up this morning and immediately remembered I needed a belt. A belt to wear around my waist to keep my pants up, I mean. What were you thinking I meant? &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_wink.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After finding one at the nearby Mark's store, I decided I was close enough to this one to make an attempt, especially since I'd seen others find it successfully recently. Well I (FOUND) it!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The tell tale divot was pretty clear when I arrived at gz and luckily when I was there I was mostly in between waves of traffic racing along Gateway Blvd.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It was a quick find and then a quick bolt back over the fence to safety as a mass of vehicles made their way toward me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the cap!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="685905329">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="53.514200" lon="-113.494233" />
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-13T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="11091667">WillowZilla</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP253 - Edmonton CPR

{found}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This was a little close to a really busy road, only one DBag honked at me from 1m away though :P Nothing really in the area at all, so hard to look inconspicuous workin' on this cap :)&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="683870686">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-05T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="7458582">mkPerry</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP253 Edmonton CPR

Third BCP over lunch today. Hardest part was crossing to the east side of the road. Once there quickly found the cap divot. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks outforthehunt for another cap. &lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="683623767">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-05-04T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="691070">UBC Geograd</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP253- Edmonton CPR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Found this one today over the lunch hour while out running a nearby errand. Cap was easy enough to find because the hole was visible from about 25 feet away. The hole was quite full of dirt and other assorted road debris, given how close to the lanes of traffic it is located. I'm pretty sure I've actually seen a surveyor here before, as I come into work this way every day, and I recall thinking it was a radar trap (which are also known to pop up around here from time to time). Given that I was probably going a little bit quick, I was relieved to see it was just a surveyor. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;E-mail sent, and pics attached. Thanks outforthehunt.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="681706648">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-04-19T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="7338377">j0ckser</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;BCP253 Edmonton-CPR

Posted: N53° 30.852 W113° 29.654

My GPSr: N53° 30.853 W113° 29.654&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GeoGuider and I met again after work to see if we could find a couple more brass caps while there’s still time. There’s been so much slushy rain/snow that this one was drowning. We scooped out a lot of water, mopped up some more, but the water table was so high only half of the cap was above water. Thanks for the brass cap, OFTH.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
